{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791361989,"nonce":"xAjy8ty5J21/mysYhs2oiMdqpdI=","title":"Inherited method entries mask child method signatures during implicit calls","message":"At aa474e08090c67e1d1e958a40ce1bf36835ed7d6, resolve_symbol_in_scope_hierarchy scans class_fields before class_methods. lower_function_from_field appends methods to class_fields, and inheritance copies those entries to the child. Consequently an inherited method is selected before the child's own method, despite the child method table containing the override.\n\nReproducer: haxe-conformance/regressions/CovariantMethod.hx (also /tmp/rayzor-method-shadow/CovariantMethod.hx). CovariantParent.values():Dynamic returns [9]. CovariantChild overrides values():Array\u003cInt\u003e to return [1,2,3], and length():Int returns values().length. Native Haxe --interp prints CONFORMANCE_OK. Baseline Rayzor throws covariant result type because the implicit call retains the parent's Dynamic result type. An ordinary same-signature override probe passes: virtual dispatch masks the wrong compile-time binding.\n\nTestIO is another instance: MIR for unit.TestIO.test calls inherited unit.Test.check(false) and check(true), rather than TestIO's own check(endian) method. This produces an assertion failure and omits the main I/O body before the other test methods run. The shim introduces this namesake, but the independent covariance reproducer establishes the compiler defect without any harness change.\n\nBaseline artifacts: /tmp/rayzor-covariant-method-baseline.log, /tmp/rayzor-method-shadow-baseline.log, /tmp/rayzor-test-io-mir-package-v1.log. Baseline full conformance is 868/1094 with 129 repository regressions passing.\n\nThe field lookup should select data fields only and leave methods to the class method table, which records overrides. Remaining I/O lowering and ABI failures are outside this issue.","files":null}]}